>
> In this article I will take a closer look at the quantifier logic=20
> currently present in (post-xorlo) Lojban. I will identify logical=20
> problems as well as practical disadvantages. Finally, I will offer a=20
> solution that addresses both.=20
>
> Not only is the current system both impractical and unintuitive, its=20
> introduction also left behind one major logical flaw as Lojban made the=
=20
> move from singular logic to plural logic.=20
>
> Read on to find out what singular logic and plural logic are, how they=20
> are related to xorlo, and why the current situation is not tenable.=20
>
> https://solpahi.wordpress.com/2016/09/25/a-simpler-quantifier-logic/=20
> <https://www.google.com/url?q=3Dhttps%3A%2F%2Fsolpahi.wordpress.com%2F201=
6%2F09%2F25%2Fa-simpler-quantifier-logic%2F&sa=3DD&sntz=3D1&usg=3DAFQjCNG35=
ZW1XitrBHzq8GPbAMyhs1Hn6Q>=20
>

One thing is that it says that under singular logic "nobody is gathering"=
=20
is impossible but why not use {selcmi}. That's of course beside the point=
=20
of the article. That section is clearly called "singular meets plural",=20
which some readers might forget about, though.

Phrasing {na ku su=E2=80=99oi jbopre na ku remna} might make the reader thi=
nk=20
whether the scope is affected by plural quantifiers or not (since {su'oi}=
=20
is in the middle but later it's asserted that plural {re} and {ci} aren't=
=20
affected by scope).

Also it'd be nice to explain one mysterious particle of class LE one day=20
since it's (for obvious reasons) absent from the proposal in this article.


And singular/plural looks like an obsession with Pythagorean number, which=
=20
rule the world.=20
E.g. such quantifier as "various" in "People talk to each other in various=
=20
languages" is roughly {so'i} and precisely based on {vrici} and=20
non-distributive (or sometimes is distributive) but is a quantifiers that=
=20
quantifies other predicates. Such quantifiers are unfortunately often=20
forgotten.

